  {
   "id": "b042",
   "page": null,
   "text": "Technical Analysis",
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "section": "Technical Analysis"
  },
  {
   "id": "b043",
   "page": null,
   "text": "The attackers behind CL-STA-1114 have been active since at least 2024, and this campaign targeting Zimbra servers started in July 2025. Initial access starts with a phishing email that contains either an HTML attachment or embedded HTML in the message text. This lure is designed to catch recipients' attention with news headlines.",
   "type": "paragraph",
   "section": "Technical Analysis"
  },
  {
   "id": "b044",
   "page": null,
   "text": "Figure 1 shows an example of the lure used and a snippet of the underlying HTML code.",
   "type": "paragraph",
   "section": "Technical Analysis"
  },
  {
   "id": "b045",
   "page": null,
   "text": "Figure 1. Example lure and a snippet of its underlying HTML content.",
   "type": "caption",
   "section": "Technical Analysis"
  },
  {
   "id": "b046",
   "page": null,
   "text": "The HTML text contains an obfuscated division with a Base64-encoded script (highlighted in red in Figure 1). The obfuscated section creates an invisible Scalable Vector Graphics (SVG) element that, upon loading, decodes the Base64-encoded script into a JavaScript payload that it injects into the victim’s browser.",
   "type": "paragraph",
   "section": "Technical Analysis"
  },
  {
   "id": "b047",
   "page": null,
   "text": "When executed, this JavaScript exfiltrates the victim’s Zimbra webmail data to a hard-coded command and control (C2) server. Exfiltrated data includes:",
   "type": "paragraph",
   "section": "Technical Analysis"
  },
  {
   "id": "b048",
   "page": null,
   "text": "CSRF tokens",
   "type": "list",
   "section": "Technical Analysis"
  },
  {
   "id": "b049",
   "page": null,
   "text": "Email address and password",
   "type": "list",
   "section": "Technical Analysis"
  },
  {
   "id": "b050",
   "page": null,
   "text": "Two-factor authentication (2FA) scratch codes",
   "type": "list",
   "section": "Technical Analysis"
  },
  {
   "id": "b051",
   "page": null,
   "text": "System and environment details",
   "type": "list",
   "section": "Technical Analysis"
  },
  {
   "id": "b052",
   "page": null,
   "text": "The victim’s last 90 days of email and search history",
   "type": "list",
   "section": "Technical Analysis"
  },
  {
   "id": "b053",
   "page": null,
   "text": "Over the course of this campaign, we observed minimal changes to the JavaScript payload.",
   "type": "paragraph",
   "section": "Technical Analysis"
  },
  {
   "id": "b054",
   "page": null,
   "text": "Figure 2 illustrates the attack chain.",
   "type": "paragraph",
   "section": "Technical Analysis"
  },
  {
   "id": "b055",
   "page": null,
   "text": "Figure 2. The attack chain.",
   "type": "caption",
   "section": "Technical Analysis"
  },
  {
   "id": "b056",
   "page": null,
   "text": "Since we began tracking this campaign, there have been at least nine IP addresses and nine domains for the C2 servers. These servers were active for an average of 35.4 days. See the Indicators of Compromise (IoC) section for a list of the IP addresses and domains used in CL-STA-1114 activity.",
   "type": "paragraph",
   "section": "Technical Analysis"
  },
  {
   "id": "b057",
   "page": null,
   "text": "Conclusion",
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "section": "Conclusion"
  },
  {
   "id": "b058",
   "page": null,
   "text": "This campaign activity in CL-STA-1114 illustrates the persistent and evolving threat of state-sponsored cyberespionage. The attacker behind this activity targets widely used mail platforms like Zimbra, posing a risk to critical industries globally.",
   "type": "paragraph",
   "section": "Conclusion"
  },
  {
   "id": "b059",
   "page": null,
   "text": "This research highlights the need for vigilance, proactive patching and advanced threat detection to protect organizations. Network administrators, defenders and security researchers should patch vulnerable systems and use the IoCs below to investigate and strengthen defenses against CL-STA-1114 and similar activity.",
   "type": "paragraph",
   "section": "Conclusion"
  },
  {
   "id": "b060",
   "page": null,
   "text": "Palo Alto Networks customers are better protected from the threats discussed above through the following products:",
   "type": "paragraph",
   "section": "Conclusion"
  },
  {
   "id": "b061",
   "page": null,
   "text": "The Cortex Advanced Email Security module routes suspicious HTML attachments to Advanced WildFire for static and dynamic analysis. This ensures that by the time an endpoint opens the attachment, it has already been scanned, allowing the agent to immediately act on a known verdict.",
   "type": "list",
   "section": "Conclusion"
  },
  {
   "id": "b062",
   "page": null,
   "text": "Advanced URL Filtering and Advanced DNS Security identify known domains and URLs associated with this activity as malicious.",
   "type": "list",
   "section": "Conclusion"
  },
  {
   "id": "b063",
   "page": null,
   "text": "If you think you may have been compromised or have an urgent matter, get in touch with the Unit 42 Incident Response team or call:",
   "type": "paragraph",
   "section": "Conclusion"
  },
  {
   "id": "b064",
   "page": null,
   "text": "North America: Toll Free: +1 (866) 486-4842 (866.4.UNIT42)",
   "type": "list",
   "section": "Conclusion"
  },
  {
   "id": "b065",
   "page": null,
   "text": "UK: +44.20.3743.3660",
   "type": "list",
   "section": "Conclusion"
  },
  {
   "id": "b066",
   "page": null,
   "text": "Europe and Middle East: +31.20.299.3130",
   "type": "list",
   "section": "Conclusion"
  },
  {
   "id": "b067",
   "page": null,
   "text": "Asia: +65.6983.8730",
   "type": "list",
   "section": "Conclusion"
  },
  {
   "id": "b068",
